{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-120155",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14093489/assets/620d47558e5ae5d054971311b7ec127a3318aaaf8ab10e773abde7bcd4f79a3c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =-y(y+2) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =y(y+2)(y-1) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & ={y}^{2}(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{\u2032}=y(y+2)(y-1).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ it also evaluates to zero when $y=2,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-y(y+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=-y(y+2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
